rcsec":0.131,"data":"survey tile 7945","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":10381.1,"note":"Found 0.1″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.12% and 0.1° in direction, 21 visits (survey tile 7945)."},{"name":"Kapteyn's Star","pm_true":8644.3,"pm_found":8643.7,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.007,"pa_error_deg":0.185,"sep_arcsec":0.258,"data":"survey tile 8545","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":8643.7,"note":"Found 0.3″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.01% and 0.2° in direction, 24 visits (survey tile 8545)."},{"name":"WISE 0855-0714","pm_true":8150.8,"pm_found":8151.7,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.011,"pa_error_deg":0.099,"sep_arcsec":0.508,"data":"survey tile 10226","ref":"Luhman & Esplin 2016","pm_measured":8151.7,"note":"Found 0.5″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.01% and 0.1° in direction, 21 visits (survey tile 10226)."},{"name":"Luhman 16","pm_true":2785.0,"pm_found":2792.9,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.285,"pa_error_deg":0.047,"sep_arcsec":1.86,"data":"survey tile 9509","ref":"Gaia DR3 position; Bedin et al. 2017 motion","pm_measured":2792.9,"note":"Found 1.9″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.28% and 0.0° in direction, 22 visits (survey tile 9509)."},{"name":"Wolf 359","pm_true":4715.3,"pm_found":4698.4,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.359,"pa_error_deg":0.007,"sep_arcsec":0.212,"data":"survey tile 6865","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":4698.4,"note":"Found 0.2″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.36% and 0.0° in direction, 20 visits (survey tile 6865)."},{"name":"Lalande 21185","pm_true":4811.7,"pm_found":4850.0,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.796,"pa_error_deg":0.019,"sep_arcsec":0.055,"data":"survey tile 1355","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":4850.0,"note":"Found 0.1″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.80% and 0.0° in direction, 18 visits (survey tile 1355)."},{"name":"Proxima Centauri","pm_true":3859.2,"pm_found":3843.4,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.41,"pa_error_deg":0.504,"sep_arcsec":0.244,"data":"survey tile 10397","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":3843.4,"note":"Found 0.2″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.41% and 0.5° in direction, 15 visits (survey tile 10397)."},{"name":"61 Cygni A","pm_true":5282.1,"pm_found":null,"recovered":false,"error_pct":null,"pa_error_deg":null,"sep_arcsec":null,"data":"survey tile 3325","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":null,"note":"No mover reported within 10″ of the star's predicted position (survey tile 3325). At W1 ≈ 2 it is far brighter than NEOWISE can measure: the single-exposure table holds only scattered-light halo detections around it (cc_flags 'H', W1 11-14), never the star itself, so no search of that table can find it."},{"name":"Epsilon Indi B","pm_true":4684.2,"pm_found":4723.7,"recovered":true,"error_pct":0.844,"pa_error_deg":0.397,"sep_arcsec":0.323,"data":"survey tile 11391","ref":"Gaia DR3","pm_measured":4723.7,"note":"Found 0.3″ from the predicted position; motion off by 0.84% and 0.4° in direction, 17 visits (survey tile 11391)."}],"summary":"Of 350 synthetic movers hidden in NEOWISE-like skies with the real NEOWISE cadence, the search found 99% of those with W1−W2 = 0 moving at least 1″/yr with W2 ≤ 13 (100% at W2 = 14.5, 96% at W2 = 15.5, 100% of bright ones moving only 0.2″/yr), measured their motion to 0.1% (median), and reported 0.00 false movers per survey tile on mover-free skies. For redder objects (W1−W2 = 2, like cold brown dwarfs) it found 97% at W2 ≤ 14.5 and 0% at W2 = 15.5. On real NEOWISE data it recovered 8 of 9 known fast-moving stars (Kapteyn's Star, WISE 0855-0714, Barnard's Star, Luhman 16, Wolf 359, Lalande 21185, Proxima Centauri, Epsilon Indi B).","method":"Injection-recovery: we build synthetic NEOWISE single-exposure detection tables that copy the real NEOWISE observing pattern (exposure times, scan ids and frames of real data, about 21 visits from 2014 to 2024), fill them with field stars, galaxies, passing asteroids and spurious detections, hide movers of known speed, direction and brightness in them, and run the survey's mover search without telling it where they are. Noise, detection limits and positional errors follow a model measured on real NEOWISE detections. A hidden mover counts as recovered when the search reports a mover within 5″ of it with a motion within 30% (or 0.1″/yr). False positives are counted on skies with no movers at all, and the search is also run on real NEOWISE data around stars whose motion Gaia DR3 measured.","limits":["Synthetic skies use high-Galactic-latitude source counts; crowded fields near the Galactic plane are not simulated at their full density.","Every exposure covers the whole synthetic patch; real frame edges and gaps are only present when injecting into real tables.","The noise model is calibrated on two real 4′ NEOWISE fields; it has no blending, no position-dependent systematics and a simple saturation model.","Injected movers have parallaxes from a tangential velocity of 80 km/s and do not accelerate (no binaries).","False-positive rates are measured on synthetic skies; real artefacts (latents, ghosts, scattered light) are more varied.","Known-star checks use small 4′ cut-outs of real data and, when available, the survey's own tile results."],"spurious_in_injected":{"tiles":10,"movers":1,"area_deg2":2.8723,"per_sky":0.1,"per_deg2":0.3482,"per_tile":1.1688,"upper95_per_tile":5.5448,"duplicates":0},"real_fields":[{"name":"Kapteyn's Star","k5":8545,"movers":1,"target_tracks":1,"other_movers":0,"seconds":0.09},{"name":"WISE 0855-0714","k5":10226,"movers":2,"target_tracks":1,"other_movers":1,"seconds":0.13}],"known_not_run":[],"grid":{"pms_arcsec_yr":[0.2,0.5,1.0,2.0,4.0,8.0,11.0],"w2":[8.0,11.0,13.0,14.5,15.5],"w1w2":[0.0,2.0],"trials":5,"n_empty":24,"seed":1,"vtan_kms":80.0,"spacing_arcmin":4.0,"density":1.0,"empty_radius_deg":0.3,"match":{"radius_arcsec":5.0,"pm_tol_frac":0.3,"pm_tol_abs":100.0}},"noise_model":{"w2_snr5_mag":14.34,"w1_snr5_mag":16.02,"sigma_arcsec_at_w2_for_w1w2_0":{"8.0":0.06,"10.0":0.062,"12.0":0.08,"13.0":0.11,"14.0":0.181,"14.5":0.249,"15.0":0.355,"15.5":0.523},"calibration":"tests/fixtures (real NEOWISE, Kapteyn's Star and WISE 0855 fields)"},"units":{"pm":"mas/yr","pm_arcsec_yr":"arcsec/yr","w2":"Vega mag","sep":"arcsec"},"core":"survey.link:process_tile","runtime_seconds":95.8},"method":"Each processed tile holds every NEOWISE single-exposure detection from 2014 to 2024 (about 20 million per tile). Detections that fail basic quality cuts (artefact flags, bad frames, moonlight, weak signal) are dropped. WISE scanned each part of the sky about every six months, so the detections of one source in one scan are averaged into a single position per visit. Those positions are linked across the ten years into tracks, including fast movers of up to 12 arcseconds per year, and a straight-line fit gives each track's proper motion and its uncertainty. Tracks seen in at least 6 visits that move at least 150 mas per year with at least 5 sigma are kept as movers. Each mover is compared with Gaia DR3 stars moved to the same date with their own motion. Every mover Gaia does not explain is then looked up in SPHEREx's all-sky spectral cube (data release 1, observed 2025-26): is a source there, where its NEOWISE motion says it should be by then, and gone from where it was in 2014? SPHEREx also gives its 0.75-5 micron spectrum, from which a methane dip at 3.3 microns and a red 3-5 micron colour (signs of a cold brown dwarf) are read as hints. Movers are then ranked: those Gaia does not explain, that are red, that SPHEREx sees where predicted and that have long, clean tracks come first. The top of the list is for people to investigate.","limits":["Only the tiles listed here have been searched; the rest of the sky has not been processed yet.","Motions slower than 150 mas per year or faster than 12 arcseconds per year are not searched.","A source must be detected in at least 6 of the roughly 20 WISE visits, so very faint or very red objects near the detection limit can be missed.","'Unexplained' means no Gaia DR3 star with a matching motion lies within 3 arcseconds. That is expected for cold brown dwarfs too faint for Gaia, but blends, artefacts next to bright stars and tracks cut at tile edges can look the same.","A track that crosses a tile edge is split between the two tiles. When both parts are found and agree in position and motion they are counted once (the longer track is kept); a part too short to be found on its own is lost.","Motions come from a straight-line fit; parallax is fitted only where a value is given, so very nearby objects can have slightly biased motions.","SPHEREx has no public source catalogue yet; its check measures the all-sky DR1 spectral cube directly at each predicted position. Its pixels are about 13 arcseconds across, so it can only see motions of more than a few pixels over 2014-2026 (roughly 1 arcsecond per year and faster); slower movers can only be shown to be there. Two sources less than a pixel apart are blended, so a spectrum can include a neighbour's light.","A candidate is not a discovery: each one needs a human check of the images and other catalogues first."],"status":"done","tiles_planned":12288,"min_pm":150.0,"unique_sources":73878932,"gaia_unchecked":0,"spherex_checked":4041,"spherex_confirmed":1}